1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:07,740
So dealing with government paperwork, it, uh, it often feels like you don't actually exist as a single human being.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:12,980
You know? You're more like a dozen fragmented paper versions of yourself.

3
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,480
Oh, completely. It's incredibly disjointed.

4
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,120
Right. You go to the health department, they want proof of who you are.

5
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:21,660
You go to the tax office, and you need that exact same proof all over again.

6
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:26,000
Yeah, and the data is constantly duplicated and rekeyed into all these siloed systems.

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:34,280
Exactly. Which is why today we are doing a deep dive into a live recorded demonstration of what is called the reference architecture.

8
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:34,720
Yes.

9
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:38,720
It's a look at the once only principle in action, which is so exciting.

10
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:48,480
It is. Our mission today is to really understand how states can transition away from that fragmented mess into a seamless digital infrastructure.

11
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:56,980
So to start, instead of forcing citizens to bring stacks of paper proof to every single office, how does this architecture actually execute the once only principle?

12
00:00:57,010 --> 00:01:00,870
Well, to understand it, we really have to look at a real world transaction.

13
00:01:01,110 --> 00:01:04,850
So in this live scenario, imagine a learner applying for a credential.

14
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:06,320
Okay. Applying where? Exactly.

15
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:09,270
At PNEA, which is the examination authority.

16
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:13,020
In the past, they'd have to bring paper proof of identity, proof of enrollment, all of that.

17
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:15,580
But here they provide just one number.

18
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,680
Just one, like their national ID.

19
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:27,660
Exactly, just their national ID. And from that, over the system's integration bus, PNEA automatically fetches the applicant's identity from PNIA,

20
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:35,280
the identity authority. Okay. And at the exact same time, it fetches their enrollment data from PLR, the learner registry.

21
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:41,000
Wait, so from just that one single question being asked, the national ID, how much is actually filled out?

22
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,180
Nine blank rows are instantly filled.

23
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:45,620
Oh, wow. That is incredibly fast.

24
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,240
Yeah. It completely eliminates that duplication we were talking about.

25
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:53,540
But look, if we have this integration bus connecting everything, I mean, it sounds a bit like a digital free-for-all.

26
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:54,260
How do you mean?

27
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:01,270
Well, once an agency plugs into this bus, are they just given like an all access pass to vacuum up any personal data they want?

28
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:05,820
Oh, no, definitely not. The system enforces strict data minimization.

29
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:09,020
It uses access lists to prevent exactly that.

30
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:10,880
So it limits what they can actually pull.

31
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:11,480
Very strictly.

32
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,530
For example, PNIA sends five necessary fields to the examination authority,

33
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,859
but it explicitly withholds three other fields it holds.

34
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:21,940
Which ones does it withhold?

35
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:23,070
The mother's name,

36
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:26,120
the birth registration number, and the address.

37
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:28,260
Really? Even though it has them on file?

38
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,700
Yes, because this specific purpose,

39
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:35,140
getting a learner credential, doesn't need them, so they aren't shared.

40
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:36,280
That makes a lot of sense.

41
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:40,720
And on top of that, just being on the bus is not permission.

42
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:48,310
If PLR, the learner registry, tries to ask the identity authority the exact same question that the examination authority just asked,

43
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:50,900
PLR gets an access denied fault.

44
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:52,960
Wait, really? Just a flat out denial.

45
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:57,320
Yep, because they don't have the explicit permission for that specific data exchange.

46
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:02,670
Okay. But with such granular controls, I have to ask about what happens when things go wrong.

47
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:03,840
Right. The resilience aspect.

48
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:12,890
Yeah, because if these systems are so strictly siloed by access rules, does the entire application just crash if one single source goes down or loses permission?

49
00:03:12,980 --> 00:03:15,030
Actually, no. It's incredibly resilient.

50
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:19,540
In the demonstration, an operator intentionally withdraws PNEA's permission.

51
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:20,940
Oh, to see what breaks.

52
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:29,280
Exactly. And when they do, the PNIA identity rows are denied, but the PLR enrollment rows still successfully fill in.

53
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:31,660
So one broken source doesn't ruin the whole form.

54
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:33,510
Exactly. And for auditing,

55
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:39,400
every single application is written to disk with each field strictly mapped to its source.

56
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:42,040
So you know exactly where every piece of data came from.

57
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:47,829
Yes. And before it goes live, the system actually passes six technical acceptance checks.

58
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:50,300
Including checking that the denial works.

59
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:53,299
Yes. Verifying the denial, checking field conformance, all of it.

60
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:55,490
It scored all green for go live approval.

61
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:57,640
That is just a massive technical triumph.

62
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:03,640
I mean, one field asked, nine fetched, purpose limited data sharing, and strictly enforced access lists.

63
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:04,700
It really changes the game.

